{#
  Ink colophon: shared frame, included by the base layout. Content comes from
  _data/site.js and _data/nav.js.

  This is the one deep pane the frame spends. A page that also composes a deep
  Section is already at the one-ink-pane-per-view cap from design.md section 1.

  The tagline's O carries the target glyph (.alt-dot). The isotype is not
  present on this pane, so nothing else is claiming circular meaning and the
  device is correct here.
#}
{% from "components/email.njk" import email %}

<footer class="cs-colophon">
  <div class="cs-colophon__grid">
    {# NAP renders from site.nap, the canonical source. Never hardcode it here:
       the same object feeds the Contact page and the JSON-LD, and they have to
       agree byte for byte.

       The email uses the obfuscated component. This is the frame, so it is the
       one that mattered most: while the colophon rendered a plain mailto, the
       address was harvestable from every page and the obfuscation on Contact
       protected nothing. The assembled link inherits paper from
       .cs-colophon__address, never madder, which measures 1.99:1 on ink. #}
    {# Postal lines and contact links are split into two blocks rather than
       run together on <br>s. On the phone the two rows have to become 44px
       touch targets with a rule between them, and a <br>-separated run gives
       nothing to size. Both spans are display:block, so the four lines render
       exactly as before at every width above the phone breakpoint. #}
    <div class="cs-colophon__identity">
    <address class="cs-colophon__address">
      <span class="cs-colophon__postal">
        {{ site.nap.streetAddress }}<br>
        {{ site.nap.cityStateZip }}
      </span>
      <span class="cs-colophon__contact">
        <a class="cs-colophon__tel" href="{{ site.nap.telephoneHref }}">{{ site.nap.telephone }}</a>
        {{ email(site.nap.emailUser, site.nap.emailDomain, "cs-colophon__mail") }}
      </span>
    </address>

    {# THE NETWORK LINK SITS BESIDE THE NAP, not in the footer nav list. It
       names the entity this studio belongs to, which is the same register as
       the address: who and where, rather than a policy or a service. It is
       OUTSIDE the <address> element on purpose, since an entity page is not
       contact information for this page.

       It reuses the colophon's existing inline link treatment rather than
       introducing one: .cs-colophon__address a already defines colour, hover
       and focus for a link on this pane, and the selector is extended to cover
       this one. #}
    <p class="cs-colophon__entity">
      <a href="{{ nav.network.url }}">{{ nav.network.label }}</a>
    </p>
    </div>

    <p class="cs-colophon__tagline">
      Get Built.<br>Get F<span class="alt-dot">o</span>und.
    </p>

    <nav class="cs-colophon__links" aria-label="Footer">
      <ul class="cs-colophon__nav">
        {% for item in nav.footer %}
          <li><a href="{{ item.url }}">{{ item.label }}</a></li>
        {% endfor %}
        {# One list item, two anchors, and the separator belongs to neither.
           See nav.js, footerPolicy. #}
        {#- Whitespace stripped between the anchors: they are inline here, so a
            newline in the template would collapse to a real space and sit
            alongside the separator's own margins. -#}
        <li class="cs-colophon__pair">
          {%- for item in nav.footerPolicy -%}
            <a href="{{ item.url }}">{{ item.label }}</a>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </div>

  <div class="cs-colophon__base">
    <span>&copy; {{ site.year }} {{ site.name }}</span>
    <span>{{ site.location }}</span>
  </div>
</footer>
